                <pb facs="0210" n="130" sameAs="frus:frus1902/pg130"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wu to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chinese Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1902-01-13"><hi rendition="#italic">January 13,
                        1902</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 225.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my note to you, dated December
                    31 last, and my conversation with you regarding the disposition of the $375,000
                    in silver bullion taken from the yamen of the salt-tax commissioner at Tientsin
                    in 1900, I have the honor to inform you that I have received a cablegram from
                    the Imperial Government indicating its preference to have the above-mentioned
                    money paid over to this legation, and authorizing me to receive the same on its
                    behalf.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wu Ting-fang.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
